  "notes": "The \"country rap tune\": TR-808 replacing the standard drum sounds, cymbals supplemented \"primarily with hi-hats\", and real blues-and-gospel harmony underneath. Two overrides carry it. First, the hat layer is `continuous` — the documented practice is a hat tier laid *over* the 808 as an extra rhythmic level, which is the opposite of crunk's hats that stop and start, and it is the single parameter that separates this from the Atlanta lane. Second, the harmony genuinely moves: i–iv–V blues motion and plagal iv–i tags with dominant sevenths at nearly half the extension weight, where the parent is 92% bare triads. The keyboard is the church organ the research names, the melody has a real 4-bar contour rather than a stab, and `countermelody` is standard rather than optional because the research says the loops carry one. ⚠ The 808 keeps `role: bassline` — the research's first claim is \"tuned 808 sub with long decay carrying the tonic\", and the played bass is named only as an addition on the soul-sample tracks, so the sub stays the low end and no second bass part is grown. ⚠ The 88–96 straight funk-forward band is NOT modelled: this entry commits to the 68–82 half-time centre of gravity (stored as its 136–164 straight equivalent), because a model that spans both feels sounds like neither. ⚠ `fills.snareRollLadder` is off — with both fill periods at 4 bars the big fill wins and the inherited ladder reads no seed, so it would write one identical fill on every one of 200. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
